#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116535 :tutorial/content "In general, the domain of a radical function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}\n$$\n\ndepends on whether $n$ (called the **index**) is even or odd.\n\nIf $n$ is *odd*, then the domain of $f(x)$ is *always* $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$. For example, the domain of\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nis $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\nWe can see this in the graph below: the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nextends across all values of $x$.\n\n![](images/c6/c64950f8f253168743e8349e3f1c4f37b3bf3ad8196ccdbc7d982b7cf337e209.png)\n\nNow, suppose that we want to find the domain of a transformed radical function, such as\n\n$$\ng(x)= -\\sqrt[3]{-x+2}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, and graph transformations will not change this interval. Therefore, we conclude that the domain of $g(x)$ is also $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "f36b7baa-b008-5800-85a0-71c8a1713b24" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4862 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116537 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to considering\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[n]{x}\n$$\n\nIf $n$ is *even*, then the domain of $f(x)$ is *always* $x\\in [0, \\infty)$. For example, the domain of\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\sqrt[4]{x}\\,\n$$\n\nis $x\\in [0, \\infty)$.\n\nWe can see this in the graph below: the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[4]{x}\n$$\n\nstarts at $x=0$ and extends forever to the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb93d815dc2a981645ea5a99248cef8f8189d4990ed269b778d691a13960fc31.png)\n\nNotice how similar this looks to the square root function! All curves of the form\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[n]{x}\n$$\n\nfor even $n$ have this shape.\n\nNow let's consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x)= 3\\sqrt[4]{x+2} -2\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3\\sqrt[4]{x+2} -2\n$$\n\nwe proceed in exactly the same way as with square roots: we set the expression under the radical sign to be greater than or equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2 \\ge 0\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\nx \\mid \\ge - 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y = g(x)$ is $x \\in [-2, \\infty)$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c0f840ea79edd03231b93156ce050b2af718b751b0f84da6b7c6dab9105e3df.png)\n\nIn general, to find the domain of a radical function of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt[n]{f(x)}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an even integer, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b35a06bf-3ad8-5d8a-899e-a094e9728c70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5156 :tutorial/title "The Domain of a Radical Function With an Even Index"}]}
